# CLAUDE.md — annotated template

Copy this to `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (global, applies everywhere) or to a repo
root (project-specific). Delete the `>` commentary as you fill it in.

> **The single rule that governs this file: facts, invariants and preferences
> only.** Recurring problems get a mechanism — a hook, a script, a cron, a CI
> gate — never a new paragraph here. Every prose rule you add competes with
> every other one for attention, and the ones that lose are invisible to you.
>
> When I audited my own sessions, the rules being violated most were the ones
> I had written most emphatically. Emphasis is not enforcement.

---

## Interaction style

> Put the thing you care about most first, and make it blunt. Mine begins
> "BE SHORT. This is the top rule." — because verbosity was the failure I hit
> daily. Be specific about format: a length cap, whether you want headings,
> how questions should be posed so you can answer fast.

- <your top preference, stated as an imperative>
- <output format you actually want>
- <how you want to be asked questions — numbered? checkboxes? one at a time?>

## Environment truths

> **The highest-value section in the file, and the one people skip.** These are
> facts the model cannot discover without failing first. Every row here is a
> failure you only have to pay for once. A table beats prose: it is scannable
> and each row is independently true.

| Thing | Truth |
|---|---|
| Bash cwd | <does it persist between calls, or reset?> |
| <tool that isn't installed> | Not available. Use <the alternative> instead. |
| <shell quirk> | <what breaks, and the working form> |
| <directory that's permission-blocked> | <what an empty result actually means> |
| <slow command> | Always run in the background |

> Mine has a dozen rows. Examples of the genre: `timeout` not existing on macOS
> without coreutils; zsh globbing eagerly on unquoted literal paths with
> brackets; SSH heredocs evaluating locally instead of remotely; TCC-blocked
> folders returning empty rather than an error. None are guessable. All were
> learned by watching something fail twice.

## Invariants

> Things that must never happen, especially destructive or outward-facing ones.
> Be concrete about the boundary — "be careful with git" is not actionable,
> "never force-push to these branches without explicit per-push approval" is.

- Never <destructive action> without <explicit condition>.
- Never <outward-facing action — send, publish, deploy> without approval.
- <credential handling: where they live, where they must never go>

## Tooling

> Which tool wins when several could do the job, and which are banned. Saves
> the model from picking a plausible-but-wrong path.

- For <task>: use <tool>, never <the tempting alternative>.
- <naming, style or library conventions you actually enforce>

## Workflow

> Where work is tracked, what gets written down, what "done" means. Be explicit
> about the finish line — "done" is where agents most often stop early.

- <issue tracker and when an issue gets created>
- <what must be true before the work is called complete>
- <what gets logged, and where>

---

## Maintaining this file

1. **When a rule keeps getting violated, that is a signal to mechanise it, not
   to re-word it.** Move it to a hook and delete the prose. See the `hooks/`
   directory in this pack for four worked examples.
2. **Prune on a schedule.** I rewrite mine every few months and cut the
   backstory: the incident that caused a rule is not the rule. Keep the
   invariant, drop the anecdote.
3. **Date structural changes.** A line noting when the file was last rewritten
   and on what principle prevents slow re-accumulation of the stuff you cut.
